.TH FMOD 3 "2026-05-06" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fmod, fmodf, fmodl, remainder, remainderf, remainderl \- floating-point remainder
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double fmod(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float fmodf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double fmodl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double remainder(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float remainderf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double remainderl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fmod ()
function computes the floating-point remainder of the division of
.I x
by
.IR y .
The result has the same sign as
.I x
(the dividend) and magnitude less than the magnitude of
.I y
(the divisor).
Specifically, for any representable values of
.I x
and non-zero
.IR y ,
.IR fmod(x, y) " = " x " \- n*" y ,
where
.I n
is the quotient of
.I x / y ,
rounded toward zero to an integer.
.PP
The
.BR remainder ()
function computes the IEEE 754 remainder of the division of
.I x
by
.IR y .
The result is computed as
.IR x " \- n*" y ,
where
.I n
is the quotient of
.I x / y
rounded to the nearest integer.  When the quotient is exactly halfway
between two integers (i.e., the fractional part is 0.5), the quotient
is rounded to the nearest even integer (banker's rounding).  The result
has the same sign as
.I x
and magnitude less than half the magnitude of
.IR y .
.PP
For most arguments, the result of
.BR remainder ()
is smaller in magnitude than the result of
.BR fmod (),
making it more accurate for subsequent calculations.
.PP
The
.BR fmodf ()
and
.BR fmodl ()
variants take and return
.B float
and
.B "long double"
values respectively.  Similarly for
.BR remainderf ()
and
.BR remainderl ().
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the remainder of the division,
with magnitude less than
.I y
(or less than
.I y /2
for
.BR remainder ()).
.PP
If
.I y
is zero, the functions return NaN and raise an invalid floating-point
exception.
.PP
If
.I x
or
.I y
is NaN, NaN is returned.
.PP
If
.I x
is \(+/-\(infinity, NaN is returned.
.PP
If
.I y
is \(+/-\(infinity and
.I x
is finite, the functions return
.I x
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIy\fR is zero
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR fmod (),
.BR fmodf (),
.BR fmodl (),
.BR remainder (),
.BR remainderf (),
.BR remainderl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fmod ()
and its variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
.BR remainder ()
and its variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The handling of special arguments follows C99 Annex F (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program demonstrates the difference between
.BR fmod ()
and
.BR remainder ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    printf("fmod(5.3, 2.2)       = %.3f\en", fmod(5.3, 2.2));
    printf("remainder(5.3, 2.2)  = %.3f\en", remainder(5.3, 2.2));
    printf("fmod(\-5.3, 2.2)      = %.3f\en", fmod(\-5.3, 2.2));
    printf("remainder(\-5.3, 2.2) = %.3f\en", remainder(\-5.3, 2.2));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fmod(5.3, 2.2)       = 1.000
remainder(5.3, 2.2)  = \-0.100
fmod(\-5.3, 2.2)      = \-1.000
remainder(\-5.3, 2.2) = 0.100
.EE
.RE
